i'm very pleased to say that the  environment secretary george houston  joins us in the studio minister lovely  to see you this morning um  i suppose one has to ask after what we  were hearing in in james report there  how concerned  is the british government about the  behavior of the u.s president right now  well look um we think that uh the the  statistics are very clear that joe biden  has won that's what uh the the position  is  but it's um this is a you know u.s  election  uh and if trump's got concerns about the  way it was done uh it's for him to  consider if there are legal options  we're not going to interfere and that is  not our job team  you have to concede though it it doesn't  look great and i understand  that you perhaps won't go as far as as  your colleague sergey javid  but you know his claim that this is  adolescent behavior does seem to have a  point  well look i'm not a returning officer in  the u.s election i don't know exactly  what uh  happened there if there were issues and  obviously those engaged in that election  i've got a right to look at that and  take that up but of the prime minister  has already  um you know welcomed joe biden to the  post issued a statement as of leaders  around the world  it does uh look pretty clear that joe  biden's won this election now he's  crossed that  uh threshold of 270 electoral college  votes and uh and you know appears to be  the next  u.s president we move on to potential  problems that we might have with with  our biden administration in a second but  there does appear to be something of an  opportunity an opening particularly for  your department with joe biden really  pushing on this  this green new deal and and we here in  the uk trying to  reposition ourselves as a leader in  green tech yes i mean obviously it'll be  a different administration with a  different focus in many areas but  i think it's fair to say on the  environmental front the  biden uh you know biden camp have been  quite clear that they're going to rejoin  the paris cop agreement and uh take a  leading role in tackling climate change  and i think that's a really  important opportunity for us we're  hosting cop 26  next october in the uk a big year on  biodiversity as well next year and i  think that this administration has  already signaled it's going to be  much more engaged in some of those  international forums for the environment  almost  almost feels like you're suggesting that  us leadership on environmental issues  has been missing for a few years  well the trump administration took a a  particular view  and on these matters um you know it's no  secret that we're not enthusiastic about  multilateral global agreements on issues  like the environment indeed they  withdrew  from some of those conventions so they  had a very clear stance on that  the biden camper suggesting a very  different approach and i think that's  probably uh right and consistent with  what the uk wants to see on the  environment  let's talk about where there may well be  problems and it certainly seems to be  the case joe biden has made it very  clear that he will not accept any  undermining of the good friday agreement  we know  uh that the internal markets bill is  going through the lords today  if various clauses are taken out by the  lords  will the government reinstate them  straight away well  and we will because the the uk internal  markets bill it's not about undermining  the belfast agreement it's about  standing behind it  uh and making sure that it works and  looking after um you know the interests  of northern ireland  making sure that the peace and stability  that's been uh hard won there can carry  on  and so the limited number of areas where  um we took a power subject to parliament  agreeing it  to be able to create legal clarity and  legal certainty should there be areas  that the joint committee process in the  uh negotiations with the eu if there are  areas they can't agree we've got to  provide that legal  certainty and legal clarity and that's  all the the bill does  in terms of the of the deal being struck  with the european union exactly where  are we and  and how much are we now apparently given  words that we heard from the government  over the weekend willing to concede on  the matter of fishing  uh well look the negotiations be going  on now for obviously many many months  they resume again this week i understand  there are still  differences there are still some  obstacles to overcome  but i think there's now you know some  good will on both sides to try to  progress things a lot of work going into  to that um  david frost and the negotiating team for  the uk working very hard to try to get  an agreement  and on fisheries and we've always um  we've always been open to doing a  a sensible approach um looking  potentially  at uh agreements that might span a  couple or three years for instance  um we're going to be sensible in how we  approach this but  making sure that we have control of our  own waters again and control access to  our waters has always been  a red line for us in these negotiations  it doesn't feel though that you're  steering way away from the idea that we  may well  be prepared to give ground on what what  to me has throughout this appeared like  a fairly obvious red line  our waters are a fish well we're not  giving ground on that i mean when you  leave the european union you do become  an independent coastal state you control  access  in your exclusive economic zone that's  out to 200 miles or the halfway point  that's what norway and pharaoh's and  every other country in the world  actually has it's just a  simple feature of international law so  that's a point of international law  rather than a matter for negotiation the  issue will become  how much uh what are the sharing  arrangements how much mutual access do  we allow in one another's waters  and that's obviously a discussion  that'll happen annually uh but there may  also be  you know a partnership agreement that  sets out the ground rules as to how we  will  work on that and we've certainly  published that and put proposals to the  eu  on your brief minister to two topics  that i never thought would uh coexist in  the one place  coveted 19 and danish minx tell us  exactly what is going on at the moment  because there is a mass cull ongoing in  denmark we have seen  uh essentially the walls being put up  between transport between there and here  is  is there anything that we need to be  concerned about well look  we don't have uh mink farming in this  country was banned  in 2002 and rightly so but some  countries in the world including in the  us  but also denmark and the netherlands as  well  do still allow mink farming and we know  that um  mink come from a family of animals the  mustelids that are  quite susceptible to covid19 uh indeed  so much so that they're sometimes used  for  uh you know experiments in this area to  understand the virus  and there has been a new strain that has  emerged in denmark and we think may also  be in the  uh netherlands as well but certainly  clear in the denmark  was it wise then for the for the the  travel ban to be announced that you know  half past one in the morning and brought  in at half past three it does always  feel  the the government's characterization of  this as a whack-a-mole strategy  sometimes is absolutely on the mark well  i think look we're responding  to um you know evolving evidence and  emerging events this is a  fast-moving virus um you know what we  realized at the end of  uh you know last week is that there had  been a new strain of the virus that had  developed in denmark and crucially  uh that had then reinfected a human from  a mink  that was a significant new development  and we felt it was important to  act expeditiously and quickly to uh to  try to  make sure there wasn't a risk of that  coming to the uk and that's all we've  done  could we talk briefly about about kate  bingham and her role um  on the coveted task force 670 thousand  pounds of taxpayers money  has been signed off for use on a team of  eight pr consultants  eight pr consultants who could have each  pointed out that spending 675 grand on  pr consultants  might not be the best idea what is going  on with ms bingham at the moment  well look i'm not involved then  obviously matt hancock  is in the department for health will be  monitoring  all of the work that they do um i don't  know whether it was strictly pr  consulted or whether it was management  consultants more generally  uh as you know neil i used to uh work in  uh  in that industry but also as preceptor  david cameron and so  you know generally i think these  resources can come from in-house but i  don't know the specific circumstances  of those particular contracts but as you  say your political antenna must surely  have been vibrating when you read about  that  in much the same way as when you read  about marcus rashford and his  campaign to to bring back free school  news just explain this one to me or  particularly on the marcus rashford  point  why did the government expend so much  political capital  on a battle that they were clearly never  going to win  on the one hand in one corner you've got  boris johnson in the other  you've got marcus rashford and poor  hungry children now you may well argue  that there is a point to be made a  debate to be had about provision outside  of term time  but the time to have that debate is  surely not in the middle of a viral  pandemic well look what we said at the  time and indeed i think last time i was  on this program  i i explained that the government didn't  think that the free school meals voucher  scheme was the right way to go because  that should be offered in term time  but there were other areas that were  under review and we we mentioned for  instance  the 63 million fund that we had for  local authorities uh we mentioned that  we were open to  uh extending that and increasing that  and looking at other ways of tackling  this problem and that's what we've  got you and your colleagues we're out in  front of the cameras you know this we're  opening the cameras  defending a decision that you probably  all expected was going to  if not a u-turn performed on it because  the the scheme that has been announced  is different to  to what has happened before but it would  look  pretty much like a u-turn well things  can be presented that way but i know and  i was involved in the discussion at the  time that  a decision was made by government the  free school meals wasn't the right way  to go but there were other things that  we could and should do in this space  those have been worked on and developed  and have been announced over the weekend  so  this is actually not a u-turn it's a  consistent approach  consistent with what we said at the time  that there were other ways that we ought  to intervene to help those in food  poverty and that's what we've done  and it's a scheme that will last all the  way till the end of next year  what about the year after that well um  look you take one year at a time on some  of these things but  um and these are obviously you know we  we refine things based on how they work  but the winter grant fund runs until  march  provides 170 million pounds to local  authorities  and then from next spring during the  easter summer and christmas holidays  next year  there's going to be increased funding  for the holiday  activities and food scheme which is all  about  supporting young people during those  holiday times run  by local authorities are very much  focused on getting to people to  understand food change the culture of  people's relationship with food  and to prepare food george used this  always good to see you thanks for  joining us thank you very much

